://data.europeana.eu/proxy/europeana/1200/039_Id_14"><dc:creator rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/agent/31495"></dc:creator><dc:date rdf:resource="#1557"></dc:date><dc:identifier>039_Id_14</dc:identifier><dc:language>lat</dc:language><dc:language>ell</dc:language><dcterms:temporal rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/timespan/16"></dcterms:temporal><edm:europeanaProxy>true</edm:europeanaProxy><edm:year>1557</edm:year><ore:proxyFor rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/item/1200/039_Id_14"/><ore:proxyIn rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/aggregation/europeana/1200/039_Id_14"/><ore:lineage rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/proxy/provider/1200/039_Id_14"/><edm:type>IMAGE</edm:type></ore:Proxy><ore:Proxy rdf:about="http://data.europeana.eu/proxy/provider/1200/039_Id_14"><dc:creator>Jacob Clauser</dc:creator><dc:creator rdf:resource="http://viaf.org/viaf/13491021"></dc:creator><dc:date>1557</dc:date><dc:description xml:lang="en">The painting shows how quotations from the Bible and the Greek Church Fathers could be used to promote a distinctive Christian identity. The Greek inscription in the lower left corner is from 1 Tim. 6, 6 ("Godliness with contentment is great gain"), which Amerbach also used to adorn his coat of arms. Another Greek inscription ("in one, all") is placed on either side of Amerbach's head and recalls Dio Chrysostom's characterisation of Paul (Hom. 2 Cor. 25.3 = PG 61:574). These words may have been added by Giovanni Bernardino Bonifacio (1517-97), who received the painting as a gift, as a tribute to Amerbach, drawing a parallel between him and the Apostle.</dc:description><dc:format xml:lang="en">Painting</dc:format><dc:identifier>Inv. 388</dc:identifier><dc:language>Greek</dc:language><dc:language>Latin</dc:language><dc:subject rdf:resource="http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q892504"></dc:subject><dc:subject xml:lang="en">Bonifacius Amerbach</dc:subject><dc:title xml:lang="en">Portrait of Bonifacius Amerbach</dc:title><dcterms:isPartO